    "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-ws1-acceptance-s1-q-1.png\" alt=\"Original workshop source extract\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>",
    "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-ws1-acceptance-s1-a-1.png\" alt=\"Original workshop source extract\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>",
    "isHTML": true,
    "searchText": "Situation 1  \nMegan is an audit manager and is preparing for her second audit on the client Really Wheely Skates \nLimited (RWS). She has seen in some press reports that RWS has suffered some problems recently. \nUse of the company’s best-selling electric skateboard, the E-Wheely, has recently been banned on \npublic roads. This has led to a decrease in profits of almost 50% in RWS’s Q2 trading update. \nFrom discussions she has had with the financial controller at RWS, Megan knows that the company is \nsuffering from significant cash-flow difficulties. Accounts Payable Days have increased from around \n29 days in the previous year to 74 days last month.  \nDue to this, the financial controller has told Megan that it is essential that the audit runs smoothly, to \nrestore the trust of the investors. Therefore, he is happy to pay a much higher audit fee than in the \nprevious year, but only if no misstatements are identified.  \n ",
