    "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-ws1-acceptance-s3-q-1.png\" alt=\"Original workshop source extract\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>",
    "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-ws1-acceptance-s3-a-3.png\" alt=\"Original workshop source extract\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>",
    "isHTML": true,
    "searchText": "Situation 3  \nKaleem is an audit manager at M&N Limited (M&N) and has been approached by a potential new \nclient, Myla Fashion Limited (Myla). During the year, Myla has become very popular in supplying \naffordable clothing in popular styles primarily to a market of 15–30-year-old women. However, the \ncompany has received some negative publicity due to concerns around their supply chain. There have \nbeen some reports of forced labour, although Myla has vehemently denied these. \nThe financial director (FD) of Myla has made it very clear to Kaleem that the audit will need to be \nfinalised as soon as possible, and that they would expect audit fieldwork to be complete three weeks \nafter acceptance of the audit. The FD and other directors are all happy to make themselves available \nto answer any questions which the audit team may have, except for one director who lives abroad. \nThe FD commented that this director is not involved in the day-to-day running of the business and that \nthey haven’t responded to any correspondence from the FD in the last six months. ",
